// ../packages/content-pf/src/guidance.generated.ts
var guidance3 = [
  {
    id: "pf1e",
    aliases: ["pf1e", "pathfinder 1e"],
    body: "# Pathfinder 1E\n\n## What is specific to this system\n\n- Base attack bonus, CMB and CMD, and three separate saves (Fortitude, Reflex, Will)\n  rather than PF2E's proficiency ladder.\n- Iterative attacks at high BAB rather than a multiple attack penalty.\n- Alignment is in play and mechanically load-bearing for some classes.\n\n## In combat\n\n- Standard, move, swift and full-round actions. A full attack is a full-round action, so\n  moving usually costs the extra attacks.\n- Attacks of opportunity are frequent; provoke deliberately and tell the player when\n  they are provoking.\n- Track it explicitly in your message: the action economy is the interesting part.\n\n## Companions and sidekicks\n\nThe action economy is the reason to have one. A lone character facing three enemies is giving up\nthree turns a round to its one, and iterative attacks only help if it can afford to\nstand still.\n\nThe system already ships several ways to add a body without a second full sheet:\nan animal companion, a familiar, or a cohort through the leadership rules for a player who\nwants a genuine second combatant. Prefer those to a bespoke sidekick, because they are\nbalanced against the encounter maths already.\n\nWhere a full ally is wanted, use a creature two or three levels below the party and\ngive it a single signature action. Keep its turn to one or two actions in practice --\nnot because the rules say so, but because a companion taking a full three-action turn\nevery round doubles the length of every fight.\n\nCompanions provoke and are provoked like anyone else, and a companion caught flat-footed\nis a good moment to let the fiction bite."
  },
  {
    id: "pf2e-legacy",
    aliases: ["pf2e (legacy)", "pf2e legacy", "pathfinder 2e legacy"],
    body: "# Pathfinder 2E \u2014 legacy\n\nThis is the printing in play. State it in one clause when you confirm the setup, and do not drift to the other one.\n\n## What is specific to this printing\n\n- The 2019 Core Rulebook. Alignment is in play, schools of magic exist, damage is\n  negative/positive, and spells have their original names.\n- Chosen deliberately over the remaster, so do not quietly modernise a rule.\n\n## In combat\n\n- Three actions per turn, plus a reaction. Most things cost one action; casting usually\n  costs two.\n- **Multiple attack penalty:** second attack in a turn is at -5, third at -10 (-4/-8 with\n  agile). This is the main reason PF2E turns are interesting; enforce it.\n- **Four degrees of success:** beat the DC by 10 for a critical success, fail by 10 for a\n  critical failure. A natural 20 improves the degree by one step, a natural 1 worsens it.\n  The dice tool reports natural 20s and 1s, so you can apply this without guessing.\n- Dying/Wounded: dying increases with each hit while down; wounded makes the next one\n  worse. Track both in the Status block.\n- Hero points: one at the start, more for good play. Spending one rerolls a check;\n  spending all of them recovers from dying. Remind the player they have them.\n\n## Companions and sidekicks\n\nThe action economy is the reason to have one. Three actions each means a lone character\nfacing three enemies is giving up nine actions a round to its three, and the multiple\nattack penalty means it cannot simply swing more.\n\nBoth printings already ship several ways to add a body without a second full sheet:\nan animal companion, a familiar with the right abilities, or an eidolon for a player who\nwants a genuine second combatant. Prefer those to a bespoke sidekick, because they are\nbalanced against the encounter maths already.\n\nWhere a full ally is wanted, use a creature two or three levels below the party and\ngive it a single signature action. Keep its turn to one or two actions in practice --\nnot because the rules say so, but because a companion taking a full three-action turn\nevery round doubles the length of every fight.\n\nDegrees of success apply to companions exactly as to anyone else, and a critical\nfailure on a companion's strike is a good moment to let the fiction bite."
  },
  {
    id: "pf2e-remaster",
    aliases: ["pf2e", "pathfinder 2e", "pf2e (remaster)", "pf2e remaster", "pathfinder 2e remaster"],
    body: "# Pathfinder 2E \u2014 remaster\n\nThis is the printing in play. State it in one clause when you confirm the setup, and do not drift to the other one.\n\n## What is specific to this printing\n\n- No alignment. Schools of magic replaced by traditions and new traits.\n- `void`/`vitality` damage instead of negative/positive.\n- Renamed spells: magic missile is *force barrage*. Reworked witch, oracle and champion.\n\n## In combat\n\n- Three actions per turn, plus a reaction. Most things cost one action; casting usually\n  costs two.\n- **Multiple attack penalty:** second attack in a turn is at -5, third at -10 (-4/-8 with\n  agile). This is the main reason PF2E turns are interesting; enforce it.\n- **Four degrees of success:** beat the DC by 10 for a critical success, fail by 10 for a\n  critical failure. A natural 20 improves the degree by one step, a natural 1 worsens it.\n  The dice tool reports natural 20s and 1s, so you can apply this without guessing.\n- Dying/Wounded: dying increases with each hit while down; wounded makes the next one\n  worse. Track both in the Status block.\n- Hero points: one at the start, more for good play. Spending one rerolls a check;\n  spending all of them recovers from dying. Remind the player they have them.\n\n## Companions and sidekicks\n\nThe action economy is the reason to have one. Three actions each means a lone character\nfacing three enemies is giving up nine actions a round to its three, and the multiple\nattack penalty means it cannot simply swing more.\n\nBoth printings already ship several ways to add a body without a second full sheet:\nan animal companion, a familiar with the right abilities, or an eidolon for a player who\nwants a genuine second combatant. Prefer those to a bespoke sidekick, because they are\nbalanced against the encounter maths already.\n\nWhere a full ally is wanted, use a creature two or three levels below the party and\ngive it a single signature action. Keep its turn to one or two actions in practice --\nnot because the rules say so, but because a companion taking a full three-action turn\nevery round doubles the length of every fight.\n\nDegrees of success apply to companions exactly as to anyone else, and a critical\nfailure on a companion's strike is a good moment to let the fiction bite."
  }
];

